<!-- One small accessibility tip: usually emojis or other "non text" unicode characters are kinda bad for screen readers.

Like, when we see this emoji :man_in_business_suit_levitating: it is just like a random dude dressed in business casual. Takes one space only, nothing weird.
A screen reader will read the actual name of the emoji: man in business suit levitating.

And that... it breaks the flow of text around it making it kinda confusing.

So, suggestions for small pictures/emojis around text:

Use an svg image instead, it also gives you more control over the image to use
Wrap the emoji in spans
Avoid scattering emojis throughout the text, mostly leaving them to the end of sentences


In the first two cases, you can add an aria-label attribute to the svg or span to describe what that image is in the context of the text around it.
Or add an aria-hidden="true" to the svg or span to hide the image if it is only for decoration (like an icon next to a word on a menu) -->
<style>
	html,
	body {
		margin: 0;
		width: 100%;
		height: 100%;
	}
</style>
